<figure class="mm-figure" data-mm-figure-id="3cfef813cbf91c60d6ac43be" data-mm-topic-id="t-mm-08-003"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="1. Previous VTE on long term oral anticoagulant therapy- unprovoked- E dependent.- idiopathic Very high risk2. Antithrombin deficiency.(Antithrombin replacement @ initiation of labor.)3. anti-phospholipid syndrome with previous VTE.4. Previous recurrent VTE.Any previous VTE (except a single VTE related to major surgery).Asymptomatic high – risk thrombophilia homozygous factor V Leiden/ compound heterozygote.Protein C or S deficiency.Single previous VTE associated with major surgery without thrombophilia, family history or other risk factors.Asymptomatic low – risk thrombophilia (prothrombin gene mutation or factor V Leiden) heterozygous.Recommend antenatal high dose (50%, 75% or full therapeutic doses &amp; at least 6 weeks’ postnatal LMWH or until switched back to oral anticoagulant therapy. (Consider x-a monitoring 0.5 – 1.)These women require special management by experts in hemostasis and pregnancy.Recommended antenatal and 6 weeks’ postnatal prophylactic LMWH.Refer to local expert.Consider antenatal LMWH.Recommended postnatal prophylactic LMWH for 6 weeks.Consider antenatal LMWH (but not routinely recommended)Recommended LMWH from 28 weeks of gestation and 6 weeks’ postnatal prophylactic LMWH.Consider as risk factor and score approximately (see appendix III). Recommended 10 days if other risk factor postpartum (or 6 weeks’ if significant family history) postnatal prophylactic LMWH.Very high riskHigh RiskIntermediate RiskLow Risk" viewbox="352425 200025 6905625 5762625" preserveaspectratio="xMidYMid meet" data-mm-object-manifest="6fb7d2417cc12d63d45b4a98e626f4d3f66fc920c1c3c04610a8f46a0beebe7d"><g data-mm-object-id="a0db7471ac5bbce3ee5e" transform="translate(1767840 231726)"><g transform="translate(0 0)"><path d="M 0 0 L 2730500 0 L 2730500 5547360 L 0 5547360 Z" fill="none" fill-opacity="0" stroke="